  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1212 AM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nToday: Cold Start to the Day - Breezy Wind This Afternoon\n\nInfrared satellite imagery depicts clear skies over the Upper \nMidwest and surface obs indicate very light winds around 10 mph or \nless are ongoing. These conditions will lead to continued \nradiational cooling with temperatures dropping down into the lower \n30s to lower 20s. In fact, freezing to sub-freezing temperatures are \nalready being observed for some locations across the area, most \nnotably in the known cold spots of central Wisconsin. Most locations \nacross the forecast area should fall to at least freezing this \nmorning, which should essentially end the growing season for this \nyear.\n\nSurface high pressure will stay to the south and east of the region \ntoday and south to southwest wind should result over the Driftless \nRegion. The sustained wind this afternoon is expected to increase up \nto 10-20 mph with gusts to 30 mph in response to the tightening \nsurface pressure gradient. The south wind will begin to usher in \nwarmer air, but due to the cold start to the day, afternoon highs \nwill be below average once again today. \n\nSunday into Next Week: Dry Conditions with Increasing Temperatures\n\nUpper level ridging will develop across much of the central CONUS by \nearly next week. This pattern will be favorable for temperatures to \nrise back up above average and for mostly dry conditions to persist. \nOutside of the continued breezy southwest wind on Sunday, the wind \nshould be light throughout the next work week. Overall, a very nice, \nlate October week is forecast for the region. Afternoon highs have \nat least a medium chance (50% or higher) of reaching 70 degrees area-\nwide by Wednesday into Thursday. If these forecast high temperatures \nare to pan out, they would roughly be 10 degrees above average for \nthis time of year. The next potential weather system to bring \nunsettled weather to the region may try to lift northeast from the \nDesert Southwest late next week or early next weekend.",
